{"id":"00b56f146c16e1813e5e6fc9106c6ff673ecd2a248e26c87291373d81cbbd78c","lamport":0,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-10-04T12:01:12.453229Z","format_version":1,"kind":"create","payload":{"fields":{"created-at":"2026-10-04T12:01:12.453229Z","created-by":"carlo.cifarelli@gmail.com","description":"Architectural decision ticket arising from the git-bug competitive teardown\n(session 2026-10-02). Today ticgit stores the *current state* of each ticket\ndirectly under `ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:<field>` and syncs those keys, so two\nclones editing the same ticket concurrently collide at merge with last-write-wins\n(LWW) clobber.\n\nProposal: make an **append-only operation log** the conflict-free source of truth\n— the only thing that syncs and merges — and repurpose the current state keys as\na **disposable, rebuildable derived cache** so `ti list` / `ti show` stay fast\nwithout replaying the log on every command.\n\nThis mirrors git-bug's own design (operation DAG as truth + separate rebuildable\ncache). It unlocks free history/blame/point-in-time queries and removes LWW\nclobber, at the cost of a replay+cache layer and a format migration.\n\nScope of THIS ticket = make the architectural decision concrete and durable\n(spec attached). Implementation is sequenced in sub-issues/follow-ups per the\nteardown roadmap; this is not a single-PR change.\n\nReference artifact: \"git-bug teardown — ideas for ticgit\"\n(claude.ai artifact c79a18d1-ced9-4d97-977a-70bfe0eec67b).\nSupporting analysis: memory obs 34312, 34313, 34314, 34330.","priority":"2","spec":"# Op-log + derived cache — architecture spec\n\n## Problem\n\nticgit stores the current state of each ticket directly as synced git-meta keys\n(`ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:<field>`). Concurrent edits in two clones merge with\nlast-write-wins: one side's change silently clobbers the other. There is also no\nnative history/blame — the keys only hold the latest value.\n\n## Decision\n\nSplit the model into two layers:\n\n1. **Operation log — the source of truth.** Append-only, conflict-free, the only\n   thing that syncs and merges between clones.\n2. **Derived cache — a disposable build artifact.** Rebuildable from the log;\n   keeps `ti list` / `ti show` fast without replaying the log per command.\n\nThis is git-bug's architecture (op DAG as truth + separate rebuildable cache)\nadapted to ticgit's git-meta storage.\n\n## Operation log layout\n\nOperations live under a per-ticket op namespace, ordered by a Lamport clock with\nthe content hash as tiebreaker (git-bug precedent):\n\n    ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:ops:<lamport>:<hash>\n\nEach op is an immutable record of a single mutation (create, set-field,\ncomment, state-change, …). Replaying the ops in (lamport, hash) order on any\nclone yields identical state — no semantic three-way merge needed.\n\n**Merge = union of op sets.** When two clones diverge, concurrent ops are simply\nunioned and replayed in clock order. No clobber: both edits survive as distinct\nops; conflicting field-sets resolve deterministically by clock order, and the\nlosing value is still recoverable from history.\n\n## Derived cache + staleness detection\n\nEach ticket's cache records the op-log head it was built from:\n\n    ticgit:tickets:<uuid>:cache:head\n\nOn every read, compare cached head vs real op-log head — three tiers:\n\n| Condition            | Head relation            | Action                        |\n|----------------------|--------------------------|-------------------------------|\n| Fresh                | heads equal              | use cache directly, zero replay |\n| Behind               | head moved forward       | replay only ops after cached head (incremental) |\n| Diverged             | heads diverge (merge)    | rebuild that ticket's cache from scratch |\n\n## Cache placement — DECISION NEEDED (recommendation: local)\n\n| Option                          | Pros                                             | Cons                                                |\n|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------|\n| **Local, gitignored** (git-bug: `.git/git-bug/cache`) | smaller push/pull; cache can never conflict; clean sync | breaks the pure all-in-git-meta story |\n| **Synced in git-meta**          | preserves all-in-git-meta purity                 | redundant data on the wire; re-introduces LWW clobber on cache keys |\n\nRecommendation: **cache is a local build artifact; only the op-log syncs.**\nPrecedent already exists — `session_state.rs` keeps local-only state outside the\nrepo, so the all-in-git-meta invariant is already not absolute.\n\n## Consistency oracle — `ti verify`\n\nFree payoff: a `ti verify` command replays the full op-log into a fresh state and\ndiffs it against the cache. Any mismatch flags cache drift, op-log corruption, or\na bad migration. Cheap to build once the two layers exist; a strong correctness\nnet for the migration itself.\n\n## Sequencing (from the teardown roadmap)\n\nDe-risk the format break by landing low-risk wins first, then the flagship:\n\n1. Content-derived IDs (SHA-256 of op content)  — low risk\n2. Format versioning per ticket + `ti migrate`  — low risk, survivable breaks\n3. Forward-compatible ops (older clients skip unknown op types) — low risk\n4. **Op-log CRDT + derived cache** (this ticket's flagship)\n5. Identity + signed ops (verified authorship across clones)\n6. Bidirectional bridges (GitHub/GitLab) — deferred, high effort\n\nSteps 1–3 make step 4's migration survivable for existing repos.\n\n## Modules touched\n\n- `keys.rs` — add the `ops:` and `cache:head` key layouts (single source of truth).\n- `store.rs` — op append + replay; cache read-through with staleness check; no index still.\n- `ticket.rs` — model ops / replay folding into the `Ticket` state.\n- new `ti verify` command in `commands/`.\n\n## Resolved decisions (2026-10-04, pending partner review)\n\n### 1. Op granularity → one compound op per user command\n\nOne operation per *logical user action*, not per field and not one giant batch.\nA `ti edit` that changes several fields emits a single compound op carrying a\n`field → value` map (git-bug's SetMetadata/EditComment style). Rationale: the op\nis the atomic unit of a user action, fewer ops to replay, and merge stays\nconflict-free — concurrent compound ops union and resolve per-field by clock\norder, so two clones editing *different* fields of the same ticket both survive.\n\n### 2. Lamport clock → embedded in each op, max derived from the scan\n\nEach op embeds its Lamport value; `<lamport>` is already in the key. The current\nmax is derived for free during the same prefix scan that builds the cache (no\nseparate index — consistent with ticgit's no-index model). Append does\n`new = max(seen) + 1`; `cache:head` memoizes the last-seen max so steady-state\nappends skip a full rescan (incremental). Concurrent equal-lamport ops tiebreak\nby content hash, which is already the second key component.\n\n### 3. Migration → single snapshot create-op per ticket\n\n`ti migrate` synthesizes ONE \"import\" op per existing ticket capturing the full\ncurrent state as a snapshot (lamport 0, authored by a synthetic migration\nidentity). We do NOT fabricate a per-field op sequence: there is no real history\nto reconstruct and a fake one would assert false provenance. Real edits append\nnormally from lamport 1 onward. Deterministic, offline, idempotent.\n\n### 4. Cache placement → local, gitignored build artifact (DECIDED 2026-10-04)\n\nThe derived cache lives locally (e.g. `.git/ticgit/cache`), gitignored; only the\nop-log syncs between clones. Chosen over synced-in-git-meta because the cache can\nthen never conflict and push/pull stay lean. Accepts breaking the pure\nall-in-git-meta story — `session_state.rs` is already a local-state precedent.\nThis unblocks the flagship (step 4) write path.","state":"new","status":"open","title":"Adopt op-log + derived cache architecture (git-bug precedent)"}}}